---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Detection Overview', href: '/docs/detection' },
  { title: 'Detection Capabilities', href: '/docs/detection/capabilities' },
  { title: 'Seeded CVEs', href: '/docs/detection/cves' },
  { title: 'Suricata Network IDS', href: '/docs/detection/suricata' },
  { title: 'Pattern Database', href: '/docs/detection/patterns' },
  { title: 'Advanced Signals', href: '/docs/detection/advanced' },
  { title: 'Device & GDPR Signature', href: '/docs/detection/signature', active: true },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'what', text: 'What It Is', level: 2 },
  { id: 'why', text: 'Why It Exists', level: 2 },
  { id: 'flow', text: 'The Flow', level: 2 },
  { id: 'privacy', text: 'Why It Is Privacy-Preserving', level: 2 },
  { id: 'recovery', text: 'Returning-Visitor Recovery', level: 2 },
  { id: 'config', text: 'Configuration', level: 2 },
  { id: 'try', text: 'See It Live', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Device & GDPR Signature" description="A consent-gated, one-way identifier that re-recognises a returning visitor without storing anything about them" section="Detection" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <h2 id="what">What It Is</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>gdpr_signature</code> is a second, <strong>privacy-preserving</strong> way for the
    reverse proxy to recognise a returning visitor — one that survives the visitor clearing
    <em>both</em> their cookies and their <code>localStorage</code>. It is layered
    <em>alongside</em> the signed <code>SERVERID</code> session cookie, never replacing it, and it
    only activates with the visitor's <strong>explicit, revocable consent</strong>. The server never
    stores anything identifiable about the visitor — only a one-way hash, and only when it matters.
  </p>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="Consent-gated" icon="check" tag="Opt-in" description="No token is generated or sent until the visitor clicks Agree. Decline, and the session cookie stays the only identifier." />
    <DocCard title="One-way" icon="fingerprint" tag="No PII at rest" description="Browser characteristics become a salted SHA-256 hash. Only the hash is stored, and only for honeypot-bound sessions." />
    <DocCard title="Deterministic" icon="route" tag="Recovery" description="The browser reproduces the same inputs live, so the same device yields the same signature after a storage wipe." />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="why">Why It Exists</h2>
  <p>
    The signed session cookie and the passive recovery fingerprint
    (<code>lua/session_identity_rules.lua</code>) can both be shed by a client that clears storage
    <em>and</em> presents a different TLS/HTTP fingerprint. The signature adds an orthogonal,
    consent-based recovery path that the browser itself reproduces deterministically, so a diverted
    adversary who wipes everything and comes back is routed straight back to the honeypot.
  </p>

  <h2 id="flow">The Flow</h2>
  <p>The source of truth is <code>ids/GDPR.md</code>; the client half lives in
  <code>gdpr_client.js</code> and the server half in <code>lua/gdpr_handler.lua</code>.</p>
  <h3>Client half (browser)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>On <code>GET /</code> the proxy injects a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> and serves
    <code>/__gdpr/config</code>. <code>gdpr_client.js</code> shows a consent banner and fetches
    <code>/__gdpr/config</code> → <code>&#123; enabled, publicKey, ... &#125;</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Only if the visitor clicks Agree</strong>, and immediately:
      <ul>
        <li>build the payload <code>&#123; s: "&lt;screenW&gt;x&lt;screenH&gt;", t: &lt;now&gt; &#125;</code>;</li>
        <li>compute <code>gdpr_token = base64( RSA-OAEP-SHA256( payload, publicKey ) )</code>;</li>
        <li>set the <code>gdpr_token</code> cookie and reload.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Server half (reverse proxy, per request, in memory only)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Decrypt: <code>plaintext = RSA-OAEP-SHA256-decrypt( gdpr_token, PRIVATE key )</code>. The
    private key never leaves the proxy.</li>
    <li>Append the client IP <strong>server-side</strong>: <code>payload.ip = $remote_addr</code> —
    the one characteristic the browser cannot supply or forge.</li>
    <li>If the timestamp is within <code>GDPR_TIMESTAMP_WINDOW</code>, build a stable canonical form
    of the characteristics (including the appended IP, timestamp stripped, keys sorted) and compute
    the one-way <code>signature = SHA256( deployment-salt | canonical )</code>.</li>
    <li>If Redis holds an unexpired <code>gdpr_honeypot_sig:&lt;signature&gt;</code>, prolong its TTL
    and route the session to the honeypot (router Stage 1a). Otherwise fall through to the normal
    session-token routing.</li>
    <li>The plaintext and characteristics are discarded immediately; only the hash may persist.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="admonition note">
    <div class="admonition-title">Binding the signature</div>
    <p>Whenever a request that carried a valid signature is routed to the honeypot for <em>any</em>
    reason, the proxy writes <code>gdpr_honeypot_sig:&lt;signature&gt;</code> with a
    <code>GDPR_BINDING_TTL</code>. A later storage wipe plus re-consent reproduces the same signature
    and lands the visitor right back on the honeypot.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="privacy">Why It Is Privacy-Preserving</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consent first, and only then.</strong> No token is generated until the visitor clicks
    Agree. Decline or never decide, and the <code>gdpr_token</code> cookie is never set; the
    <code>SERVERID</code> session stays the only identifier.</li>
    <li><strong>The browser is the only thing that can produce a token.</strong> The plaintext
    characteristics are recomputed live each time; only ciphertext is ever at rest, and it cannot be
    decrypted without the private key, which never leaves the proxy.</li>
    <li><strong>The server never stores PII.</strong> The token is decrypted only in memory, only long
    enough to verify the timestamp and compute a <strong>one-way</strong> SHA-256 hash. Only that hash
    is stored, and only for honeypot-bound signatures, as a Redis key with a TTL.</li>
    <li><strong>Revocation is "just stop sending it."</strong> The only server-side record is a one-way
    hash that expires on its own, so there is nothing identifiable to delete.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="recovery">Returning-Visitor Recovery</h2>
  <p>
    Because the signature is derived from stable device characteristics plus the server-observed IP,
    the same device from the same screen size reproduces the same signature. That is how a returning
    visitor is re-recognised <em>without</em> storing anything about them: the honeypot binding keyed
    on the hash is still in Redis, so the visitor is diverted again before the normal session routing
    even runs. Change the screen size (or the IP), and the signature changes — this is a recovery
    path, not a precise fingerprint.
  </p>

  <h2 id="config">Configuration</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>GDPR_TIMESTAMP_WINDOW</code></td><td>How fresh a token's timestamp must be for the proxy to derive a signature from it, bounding replay of a captured token.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>GDPR_BINDING_TTL</code></td><td>How long a <code>gdpr_honeypot_sig:&lt;signature&gt;</code> binding lives in Redis before it expires on its own.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>/__gdpr/config</code> <code>enabled</code></td><td>Whether the consent banner and token flow are served to the browser at all.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The signature only ever adds to honeypot routing; it is never used to send a session <em>back</em>
  to production, and it is independent of the <a href="/docs/detection/advanced">risk score</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="try">See It Live</h2>
  <p>
    The dashboard's <strong>Signature Simulation</strong> page reproduces the whole flow against the
    running proxy: resize the visitor window, press <em>Agree &amp; derive signature</em>, and watch the
    hex signature the proxy logs. Resize to a new size for a different signature, then resize back to
    the first size to see the original signature — and the re-recognition — return.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
